+### Why the asymmetric cache needs the ephemeral worker
+
+P2 and P4 are two halves of one argument, not independent phases. If PR jobs
+can write the shared cache, a hostile PR poisons an artifact a later `main`
+build consumes — supply-chain compromise by way of the build cache. The fix is
+a read-write token for `main` and a read-only token for PRs. That asymmetry is
+only enforceable on one-job-one-VM workers: on a shared persistent runner a
+write token can outlive its job on disk and be picked up by whatever runs next.
+Ephemeral workers are what make the asymmetric cache trustworthy.
+
+### Measured startup budget
+
+Probe run `31352410248` took **77 seconds** from instance launch to first job
+step. That time is spent on EC2 boot, four sequential `dnf` rounds installing
+docker, git, jq, curl and the CloudWatch agent, and a 226 MB runner tarball
+pulled from S3 and extracted — repeated on every job. P3 bakes all of it,
+targeting roughly 25–35s where boot and agent registration dominate. Going
+below that needs `pool_config`, which trades idle cost for instant pickup: one
+idle `m7i.2xlarge` on spot is roughly $70–110/mo against a $100 projection, so
+a business-hours schedule is the affordable shape, not a 24/7 pool.
